/* Microbiology 2: Immunology */
Z("micro2",0,"m",["Immunity is the body's ability to resist infection; it can be innate (inborn, non-specific) or acquired (specific, with memory). Acquired immunity can be active (body makes its own antibodies) or passive (ready-made antibodies given).","Active immunity is learning to fight yourself — slow but long-lasting; passive immunity is hiring bodyguards — instant but temporary.",["Innate: skin, mucosa, phagocytes, complement, NK cells, fever, interferon.","Active natural: after infection; active artificial: vaccination.","Passive natural: maternal IgG via placenta, IgA in breast milk; passive artificial: antiserum, immunoglobulin, ATS, anti-rabies Ig.","Active: lag period, long-lasting, memory; passive: immediate, short-lived, no memory."],"Active = Acquire by own effort; Passive = Present given."],
"Immunity is resistance to infection and to foreign substances. Innate (non-specific) immunity is present from birth: anatomical barriers (skin, mucous membranes, cilia), physiological factors (pH, temperature, lysozyme), phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages), NK cells, complement, acute-phase proteins, interferons and inflammation; it is rapid, not specific and lacks memory. Acquired (adaptive) immunity develops after exposure, is specific, has memory and is mediated by B cells (antibodies) and T cells. It may be active — the host produces its own antibodies and effector cells after natural infection (active natural) or vaccination (active artificial) — or passive — preformed antibodies are transferred naturally (maternal IgG across placenta, IgA in colostrum) or artificially (antitoxins, immunoglobulins, monoclonal antibodies). Combined (active + passive) immunisation is used in tetanus, rabies and hepatitis B exposures.",
[["Types of immunity",[["Type","Natural","Artificial"],["Active","Clinical/subclinical infection","Vaccines (live, killed, toxoid)"],["Passive","Maternal IgG (placenta), IgA (milk)","Antitoxins, immunoglobulin, monoclonal antibodies"]]],
["Active vs passive immunity",[["Feature","Active","Passive"],["Antibody produced by","Host itself","Another person/animal"],["Onset","Lag of 1–2 weeks","Immediate"],["Duration","Long (years)","Short (weeks–months)"],["Memory","Present (booster effect)","Absent"],["Negative phase","May occur","No"],["Use","Prophylaxis","Immediate protection, treatment, immunodeficient"],["Examples","BCG, MMR, TT","ATS, HRIG, HBIG, IVIG"]]],
["Innate vs acquired",[["Feature","Innate","Acquired"],["Specificity","Non-specific (PAMPs)","Highly specific"],["Memory","None","Present"],["Speed","Immediate","Days"],["Cells","Phagocytes, NK cells","Lymphocytes"]]]],
{c:"Understanding active and passive immunity guides vaccination and post-exposure prophylaxis; combined immunisation gives both immediate and lasting protection."});

Z("micro2",1,"m",["An antigen is any substance that the immune system recognises and responds to; how antigenic it is depends on foreignness, size, chemical complexity and other factors. Superantigens switch on huge numbers of T cells at once.","An antigen is a 'face' the immune system recognises; a superantigen is a master key that opens many T-cell doors at once, causing a massive alarm.",["Complete antigen (immunogen) vs hapten (needs a carrier protein to be immunogenic).","Factors: foreignness, size (> 10 kDa), chemical complexity (proteins best), stability, dose, route, adjuvants, host genetics.","Epitope = antigenic determinant; paratope = antibody binding site.","Superantigens bind outside the groove of MHC II and Vβ of TCR — TSST-1, staph enterotoxins, streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins → toxic shock."],"Antigenicity factors 'FSCD': Foreignness, Size, Complexity, Dose (+ route, adjuvant, host)."],
"An antigen is a substance that binds specifically to antibodies or T-cell receptors; an immunogen is one that can induce an immune response by itself. Haptens are low-molecular-weight substances (drugs like penicillin, dinitrophenol) that are antigenic but not immunogenic unless coupled to a carrier protein. The smallest unit recognised is the epitope (antigenic determinant). Antigens may be T-dependent (proteins; need T-helper cells, class switching, memory) or T-independent (polysaccharides; IgM, no memory). Factors influencing immunogenicity: foreignness (more distant species more immunogenic), molecular size (> 10,000 Da best), chemical nature and complexity (proteins > polysaccharides > lipids, nucleic acids), stability and degradability, physical form (particulate > soluble), dose and route, adjuvants, and host factors (age, genetics/MHC). Types: heterophile antigens (shared by unrelated species — Paul–Bunnell, Weil–Felix, Forssman), isoantigens (blood groups, HLA), autoantigens and sequestered antigens. Superantigens (staphylococcal enterotoxins, TSST-1, streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins) bind directly to MHC class II outside the peptide groove and to the Vβ region of the TCR, activating up to 20% of T cells, producing massive cytokine release and toxic shock syndrome.",
[["Factors affecting antigenicity",["Foreignness — key requirement","Size: > 10 kDa; < 5 kDa usually not immunogenic","Chemical complexity: proteins most, then polysaccharides","Susceptibility to processing (D-amino acid polymers are poor)","Dose, route (IV/SC vs oral), adjuvants","Host: age, genetics (MHC), nutrition"]],
["Types",[["Type","Example"],["Complete antigen","Proteins, bacteria"],["Hapten","Penicillin, drugs, DNP"],["Heterophile","Forssman, Weil–Felix (Proteus OX), Paul–Bunnell"],["Isoantigen","ABO, HLA"],["Sequestered (hidden)","Lens protein, sperm"],["Superantigen","TSST-1, enterotoxins, SpeA"]]],
["Superantigens vs conventional antigens",[["Feature","Conventional","Superantigen"],["Processing","Required","Not required"],["MHC binding","Peptide groove","Outside groove"],["TCR binding","Specific α and β","Vβ region only"],["T cells activated","0.001–0.01%","Up to 20%"],["Result","Specific response","Cytokine storm, toxic shock"]]]],
{c:"Antigenicity depends on properties of the antigen and host; superantigens bypass normal specificity and cause dangerous cytokine storms."});

Z("micro2",2,"m",["Immunoglobulins are antibodies — Y-shaped proteins made by plasma cells. IgG protects the body and fetus, IgA guards mucosal surfaces, IgM is the first responder, and IgE triggers allergy and fights worms.","Antibody classes are different police units: IgM the first patrol, IgG the main army (can enter the fetus), IgA the border guards in secretions, IgE the alarm system for allergy and parasites.",["IgG: 75–80%, crosses placenta, opsonisation, complement, secondary response, 4 subclasses.","IgA: secretory dimer with J chain and secretory component; in milk, saliva, tears, gut.","IgM: pentamer, largest, first in primary response, best complement activator, IgM in neonate = intrauterine infection.","IgE: lowest level, binds mast cells (Fc ε RI), type I hypersensitivity, antihelminthic."],"Ig classes 'GAMED': G most, A mucosal, M first, E allergy, D on B cells."],
"Immunoglobulins are glycoproteins with antibody activity, produced by plasma cells. The basic structure has two identical heavy and two identical light chains (κ or λ) linked by disulphide bonds, with variable regions (Fab, antigen binding) and constant regions (Fc, effector functions); papain gives 2 Fab + 1 Fc, pepsin gives F(ab')2. Five classes by heavy chain: IgG (γ), IgA (α), IgM (μ), IgD (δ), IgE (ε). IgG: monomer, 75–80% of serum Ig, half-life 23 days, crosses placenta, produced in secondary response, opsonises, activates complement (IgG3 > IgG1), neutralises toxins and viruses, mediates ADCC. IgA: serum IgA monomer; secretory IgA is a dimer with J chain and secretory component, present in colostrum, saliva, tears, intestinal and respiratory secretions; provides mucosal immunity. IgM: pentamer with J chain (MW 900 kDa), first antibody in primary response and in fetal life, most efficient complement activator and agglutinator; IgM in neonatal serum indicates congenital infection; isohaemagglutinins are IgM. IgE: monomer, lowest serum concentration, heat-labile, binds Fc ε RI on mast cells and basophils, mediates type I hypersensitivity and defence against helminths. IgD: on surface of naive B cells (antigen receptor).",
[["Properties",[["Property","IgG","IgA","IgM","IgE"],["Structure","Monomer","Monomer/dimer (secretory)","Pentamer","Monomer"],["Serum %","75–80","10–15","5–10","0.004"],["Half-life (days)","23","6","5","2"],["Placental transfer","Yes","No","No","No"],["Complement","Yes (classical)","Alternative (aggregated)","Strongest","No"],["Main function","Secondary response, opsonisation, neutralisation","Mucosal immunity","Primary response, agglutination","Allergy, helminths"],["Special","Maternal immunity","Colostrum, secretory component","Neonatal infection marker, isohaemagglutinins","Binds mast cells"]]],
["Structure",["2 heavy + 2 light chains, disulphide bonds","Variable (VH, VL) and constant domains; hypervariable regions (CDRs) form paratope","Hinge region (flexibility) in IgG, IgA, IgD","Papain → 2 Fab + Fc; pepsin → F(ab')2"]]],
{c:"Each immunoglobulin class has a distinct structure and function; their measurement aids diagnosis of infections, immunodeficiencies and allergies."});

Z("micro2",3,"m",["Precipitation is the reaction in which soluble antigen and antibody combine to form a visible insoluble precipitate; it occurs best at optimal proportions, and excess antibody or antigen inhibits it (prozone/postzone).","Like building a lattice of LEGO bricks: if pieces of one type are too many, the lattice cannot form and nothing settles.",["Lattice hypothesis (Marrack): multivalent antigen and bivalent antibody cross-link to form a lattice.","Zone of equivalence: maximum precipitate; prozone = antibody excess; postzone = antigen excess.","Methods: ring test, slide/tube flocculation (VDRL), immunodiffusion (Ouchterlony, Elek), immunoelectrophoresis, CIEP, rocket electrophoresis.","Uses: VDRL, Elek test for diphtheria toxigenicity, Ascoli thermoprecipitin for anthrax, Lancefield grouping."],"Precipitation needs 'just right' proportions (Goldilocks)."],
"Precipitation is a reaction in which a soluble antigen reacts with its antibody in the presence of electrolytes at suitable temperature and pH to form an insoluble visible precipitate. When the precipitate remains suspended as floccules, it is flocculation. Mechanism: Marrack's lattice hypothesis — multivalent antigens and bivalent antibodies form a three-dimensional lattice; large lattices precipitate. In a precipitin curve (Heidelberger), as increasing antigen is added to constant antibody, precipitation rises to a maximum at the zone of equivalence (no free antigen or antibody), then falls. Prozone: in antibody excess, each antigen is coated by separate antibody molecules and lattices fail to form, giving false negatives (seen in secondary syphilis with VDRL, brucellosis) — overcome by diluting serum. Postzone: antigen excess. Precipitation is more sensitive for antigen detection than antibody detection. Techniques: ring test (Ascoli thermoprecipitin for anthrax, Lancefield grouping of streptococci), slide test (VDRL), tube test (Kahn), immunodiffusion — single diffusion in one dimension (Oudin), double diffusion in one dimension (Oakley–Fulthorpe), single radial diffusion (Mancini — Ig quantitation), double diffusion in two dimensions (Ouchterlony — identity, partial identity, non-identity), Elek gel precipitation (toxigenicity of C. diphtheriae); immunoelectrophoresis (myeloma proteins), counter-current immunoelectrophoresis (rapid antigen detection — meningococcal, HBsAg), rocket electrophoresis.",
[["Zones",[["Zone","Condition","Result"],["Prozone","Antibody excess","No/weak precipitate (false negative)"],["Equivalence","Optimal proportion","Maximum precipitate"],["Postzone","Antigen excess","Soluble complexes, no precipitate"]]],
["Techniques and applications",[["Technique","Application"],["Ring test","Ascoli (anthrax), Lancefield grouping"],["Slide flocculation","VDRL for syphilis"],["Tube flocculation","Kahn test"],["Single radial immunodiffusion","Serum IgG, IgA, IgM, complement"],["Ouchterlony double diffusion","Antigen identity; fungal antibodies"],["Elek test","Toxigenicity of C. diphtheriae"],["Immunoelectrophoresis","Myeloma proteins, immunodeficiency"],["CIEP","Rapid antigen detection (CSF, HBsAg)"],["Rocket electrophoresis","Antigen quantitation"]]],
["Ouchterlony patterns",["Identity: lines fuse (arc)","Non-identity: lines cross","Partial identity: spur formation"]]],
{i:"Antigen–antibody reactions in vitro (serological reactions) are used to diagnose infections by detecting antibodies or antigens; precipitation was among the first to be described.",x:[["Characteristics of Ag–Ab reactions",["Specific","Involve whole molecules but not denaturation","Non-covalent bonds (hydrogen, ionic, van der Waals, hydrophobic)","Reversible","Occur in proportions; affinity and avidity determine strength"]]],f:"Soluble Ag + Ab (+ electrolytes)\n → cross-linking (lattice) at equivalence → visible precipitate\n Ab excess → prozone (false negative) → dilute serum\n Ag excess → postzone",c:"Precipitation tests, from simple VDRL to immunoelectrophoresis, remain useful; awareness of the prozone phenomenon prevents false-negative reports."});

Z("micro2",4,"m",["Agglutination is the clumping of particulate antigens (cells or particles) by their specific antibodies, used widely to detect antibodies or antigens.","Antibodies act like double-sided glue linking many particles together into visible clumps.",["More sensitive than precipitation; particulate antigen.","Slide agglutination: blood grouping, serotyping (Salmonella, Vibrio).","Tube agglutination: Widal (typhoid), Weil–Felix (rickettsia), standard agglutination test (brucella), Paul–Bunnell.","Passive (latex/RBC-coated) agglutination: RA factor, CRP, ASO, TPHA; Coombs (antiglobulin) test detects incomplete antibodies."],"Agglutination = 'clumping of cells'; Widal, Weil–Felix, Coombs."],
"Agglutination is the reaction in which particulate antigens (bacteria, red cells, latex particles) are clumped by specific antibodies (agglutinins) in the presence of electrolytes. It is more sensitive than precipitation. IgM is the most efficient agglutinating antibody. Prozone and blocking (incomplete) antibodies can cause false negatives. Types: slide agglutination (rapid; serotyping of Salmonella, Shigella, V. cholerae; blood grouping); tube agglutination (quantitative, titres — Widal test for enteric fever with O and H antigens; Weil–Felix test using Proteus OX19, OX2, OXK for rickettsial infections; standard tube agglutination for brucellosis; Paul–Bunnell for IM); heterophile agglutination; passive (indirect) agglutination using antigen-coated carriers (latex — RA factor, CRP, ASO; RBC — TPHA, Rose–Waaler test); reverse passive agglutination with antibody-coated particles to detect antigens (rapid tests for meningococcus, cryptococcal antigen); haemagglutination (direct, viral haemagglutination by influenza is non-immunological; haemagglutination inhibition); antiglobulin (Coombs) test — direct (antibody on red cells in vivo: AIHA, HDN, transfusion reaction) and indirect (antibody in serum: cross-matching, antenatal antibody screen); co-agglutination using protein A–rich S. aureus coated with antibody.",
[["Types and applications",[["Type","Applications"],["Slide","Blood grouping, serotyping Salmonella, Shigella, Vibrio"],["Tube","Widal, Weil–Felix, brucella SAT, Paul–Bunnell"],["Latex agglutination","RA factor, CRP, ASO, cryptococcal antigen"],["Passive haemagglutination","TPHA"],["Coombs (antiglobulin) test","Direct: AIHA, HDN; Indirect: cross-match, antenatal screening"],["Co-agglutination","Rapid antigen detection"],["Haemagglutination inhibition","Influenza, rubella antibodies"]]],
["Widal test",["O antigen (somatic) and H antigen (flagellar) of S. Typhi, Paratyphi A, B","Rising titre (4-fold) in paired sera diagnostic; single titre O ≥ 1:160 suggestive in endemic areas","False positive: prior vaccination, anamnestic reaction, other infections"]]],
{c:"Agglutination tests are simple, inexpensive and widely used; their interpretation requires attention to titres, paired sera and the prozone phenomenon."});

Z("micro2",5,"m",["Complement fixation test detects antigen–antibody reaction by showing that complement has been 'used up'; if complement is consumed, the indicator sheep RBCs do not lyse.","Like a test where a shared resource (complement) is used up by a first reaction, leaving none for the second reaction (RBC lysis) — no lysis means positive.",["Two systems: test (antigen + patient serum + complement) and indicator (sheep RBC + haemolysin).","No haemolysis = positive (complement fixed by Ag–Ab); haemolysis = negative.","Patient serum heated at 56 °C for 30 min to inactivate own complement.","Wassermann reaction for syphilis (historical); now largely replaced by ELISA."],"CFT: 'No lysis = Yes antibody'."],
"Complement fixation test is based on the ability of antigen–antibody complexes to fix (consume) complement. It is a two-stage test. Stage 1 (test system): patient's serum (heat-inactivated at 56 °C for 30 minutes to destroy its own complement) is incubated with known antigen and a measured amount of complement (guinea pig serum); if specific antibody is present, Ag–Ab complexes form and fix complement. Stage 2 (indicator system): sensitised sheep red blood cells (sheep RBC + amboceptor/haemolysin) are added; if complement has been fixed in the first stage, no complement is available and RBCs are not lysed — a positive result. If no antibody was present, free complement lyses the RBCs — a negative result. Controls are essential for anticomplementary sera. Applications: Wassermann reaction (syphilis, historical), diagnosis of viral, rickettsial, mycoplasmal and fungal infections. Variants: indirect CFT, conglutinating CFT, immune adherence. Largely replaced now by ELISA.",
[["Interpretation",[["Result","Haemolysis","Meaning"],["Positive","Absent","Antibody present, complement fixed"],["Negative","Present","No antibody, complement free to lyse RBCs"]]],
["Components",["Test serum (inactivated)","Antigen","Complement (guinea pig serum)","Sheep RBC + haemolysin (amboceptor)"]]],
{c:"CFT is a sensitive classical serological test that illustrates complement fixation, though modern assays have replaced it in routine use."});

Z("micro2",6,"m",["Immunofluorescence uses antibodies tagged with fluorescent dyes so that antigens or antibodies glow under a fluorescence microscope.","Like marking a target with glow-in-the-dark paint: wherever the tagged antibody binds, it shines.",["Dyes: fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC, apple green), rhodamine (orange-red).","Direct IF: labelled antibody detects antigen in tissue/smear (rabies in corneal smears, skin biopsies).","Indirect IF: unlabelled patient antibody + labelled anti-human Ig — more sensitive; ANA, FTA-ABS for syphilis.","Uses: autoantibodies (ANA patterns), syphilis, rabies, Legionella, Pneumocystis, renal biopsy deposits."],"Direct = labelled antibody directly; Indirect = labelled anti-antibody (sandwich)."],
"Immunofluorescence is the use of antibodies conjugated with fluorescent dyes (fluorochromes) such as fluorescein isothiocyanate (apple-green fluorescence) or rhodamine (orange-red) to detect antigens or antibodies, viewed under a fluorescence microscope with UV/blue excitation. Direct immunofluorescence: labelled specific antibody is applied directly to tissue or smear to detect antigen — rabies antigen in corneal impressions, skin and brain; Legionella, Chlamydia, Pneumocystis, immune deposits in kidney and skin biopsies. Indirect immunofluorescence: unlabelled antibody (patient's serum or specific antibody) is applied to known antigen, followed by fluorescein-labelled anti-human globulin; it is more sensitive (signal amplification) and one labelled reagent serves many tests — used for antinuclear antibodies (homogeneous, speckled, nucleolar, rim patterns), FTA-ABS for syphilis, autoantibodies, and viral antibodies. Sandwich technique detects antibody in cells. Flow cytometry uses fluorescent antibodies to analyse cell surface markers.",
[["Direct vs indirect",[["Feature","Direct","Indirect"],["Labelled reagent","Specific antibody","Anti-immunoglobulin"],["Detects","Antigen","Antibody (or antigen)"],["Sensitivity","Lower","Higher (amplification)"],["Examples","Rabies antigen, renal biopsy deposits","ANA, FTA-ABS, viral antibodies"]]]],
{c:"Immunofluorescence provides rapid visual localisation of antigens and antibodies and remains important in autoimmune and infectious disease diagnosis."});

Z("micro2",7,"m",["ELISA (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay) detects antigens or antibodies using an enzyme-labelled antibody that produces a coloured product; colour intensity shows the amount.","Like a 'colour-change' detective test: if the suspect (antigen/antibody) is present, the enzyme tag turns the substrate coloured.",["Types: direct, indirect (detects antibody — HIV screening), sandwich (detects antigen — HBsAg, NS1), competitive.","Enzymes: horseradish peroxidase, alkaline phosphatase; substrate gives colour read by ELISA reader.","Advantages: sensitive, specific, quantitative, automation, no radioactivity.","Uses: HIV, HBV, HCV, dengue NS1/IgM, typhoid, hormones, drugs."],"ELISA types 'DISC': Direct, Indirect, Sandwich, Competitive."],
"Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay is a solid-phase immunoassay in which an enzyme attached to an antibody or antigen converts a colourless substrate into a coloured product measured spectrophotometrically. Antigen or antibody is adsorbed onto polystyrene microtitre wells; unbound material is washed off at each step. Types: direct ELISA (labelled primary antibody detects antigen); indirect ELISA — antigen coated on well, patient serum added, then enzyme-labelled anti-human globulin and substrate; used to detect antibodies (HIV antibody screening, HCV, dengue IgM in MAC-ELISA capture format); sandwich (capture) ELISA — antibody coated on well captures antigen from specimen, then enzyme-labelled second antibody; used for antigens (HBsAg, dengue NS1, rotavirus, p24); competitive ELISA — specimen antigen competes with labelled antigen, colour inversely proportional (used for small molecules and some HBV markers). Enzymes: horseradish peroxidase (substrate TMB/OPD), alkaline phosphatase (pNPP). Advantages: high sensitivity and specificity, quantitative, safe (no radioactivity), automation, reagents stable. Fourth-generation HIV ELISA detects both p24 antigen and antibodies, shortening the window period. ELISA is a screening test; positives are confirmed by more specific tests.",
[["Types",[["Type","Detects","Principle","Example"],["Indirect","Antibody","Ag on plate → patient Ab → enzyme-anti-Ig → substrate","HIV, HCV antibodies"],["Sandwich","Antigen","Capture Ab → Ag → enzyme-labelled Ab → substrate","HBsAg, NS1, p24"],["Competitive","Antigen/antibody","Sample competes with labelled reagent; colour inversely related","Hormones, anti-HBc"],["IgM capture (MAC)","IgM antibody","Anti-µ on plate captures IgM","Dengue, JE, hepatitis A IgM"]]],
["Advantages",["Sensitive (ng/mL), specific","Quantitative, objective reading","Automated, high throughput","Safe, cheap, long shelf-life"]],
["Limitations",["False positives (cross-reactions) — needs confirmation","Window period in early infection","Requires reader and trained staff"]]],
{i:"ELISA replaced radioimmunoassay and is now the most widely used immunoassay in clinical laboratories and blood banks.",x:[["Steps of indirect ELISA",["Coat wells with antigen; block","Add patient serum; incubate; wash","Add enzyme-conjugated anti-human Ig; incubate; wash","Add chromogenic substrate; stop reaction","Read optical density; compare with cut-off"]]],f:"Plate coated with Ag (or capture Ab)\n → sample → wash → enzyme-labelled Ab → wash\n → substrate → colour → OD reading (∝ amount)",c:"ELISA's sensitivity, safety and automation make it the workhorse for screening infections such as HIV, hepatitis and dengue; positive screens are confirmed with specific tests."});

Z("micro2",8,"m",["Complement is a set of blood proteins that, once activated in a cascade, punch holes in microbes, coat them for eating and attract white cells. It is activated by classical, alternative and lectin pathways.","A row of dominoes: the first piece is tipped by antibody (classical), by microbe surfaces directly (alternative) or by sugar-binding lectin; all end in a membrane attack complex that bursts the target.",["Classical: antigen–antibody (IgM, IgG) → C1qrs → C4, C2 → C3 convertase C4b2a.","Alternative: microbial surfaces, LPS, IgA → C3b, factor B, D, properdin → C3bBb.","Lectin: mannose-binding lectin on microbes → MASP → C4, C2.","All converge on C3 → C5 → membrane attack complex C5b–9; effects: lysis, opsonisation (C3b), inflammation (C3a, C5a anaphylatoxins), chemotaxis (C5a), immune complex clearance."],"C3b = opsonin; C3a, C5a = anaphylatoxins; C5b-9 = MAC (holes)."],
"Complement is a system of about 30 serum and membrane proteins that complements antibody activity. Activation occurs as an enzymatic cascade by three pathways. Classical pathway: initiated by antigen–antibody complexes (IgM most efficient, IgG1 and IgG3); C1q binds Fc, activating C1r and C1s, which cleave C4 and C2 to form the C3 convertase C4b2a; addition of C3b forms C5 convertase C4b2a3b. Alternative pathway: activated without antibody by microbial surfaces, endotoxin, aggregated IgA, cobra venom factor; spontaneous C3 hydrolysis yields C3b, which binds factor B; factor D cleaves B to form C3bBb (stabilised by properdin) as C3 convertase, and C3bBb3b as C5 convertase. Lectin pathway: mannose-binding lectin or ficolins bind microbial carbohydrates and activate MASP-1/2, which cleave C4 and C2 like C1. All pathways cleave C5; C5b combines with C6, C7, C8 and multiple C9 to form the membrane attack complex (MAC), creating pores and lysis. Biological effects: cytolysis, opsonisation (C3b, iC3b), anaphylatoxins (C3a, C4a, C5a — mast cell degranulation, increased permeability), chemotaxis (C5a), immune complex clearance (CR1 on RBC), B-cell activation (C3d–CR2), virus neutralisation. Regulation by C1 inhibitor, factor H, factor I, DAF (CD55), CD59, C4-binding protein. Deficiencies: C1 inhibitor (hereditary angioedema), C3 (recurrent pyogenic infections), C5–C9 (recurrent Neisseria infections), DAF/CD59 (PNH), early components (SLE).",
[["Pathways",[["Feature","Classical","Alternative","Lectin"],["Initiator","Ag–Ab (IgM, IgG)","Microbial surfaces, LPS, IgA","MBL binding mannose"],["First components","C1q, r, s","C3, factor B, D, properdin","MBL, MASPs"],["C3 convertase","C4b2a","C3bBb","C4b2a"],["C5 convertase","C4b2a3b","C3bBb3b","C4b2a3b"],["Immunity","Adaptive","Innate","Innate"]]],
["Biological effects",[["Fragment","Function"],["C3b","Opsonisation, immune complex clearance"],["C3a, C5a (C4a)","Anaphylatoxins — mast cell degranulation"],["C5a","Chemotaxis of neutrophils"],["C5b–9","Membrane attack complex — lysis"],["C3d","B-cell co-stimulation (CR2)"]]],
["Complement deficiencies",[["Deficiency","Consequence"],["C1 inhibitor","Hereditary angioedema"],["C1, C2, C4","SLE-like immune complex disease"],["C3","Severe recurrent pyogenic infections"],["C5–C9","Recurrent Neisseria infections"],["DAF/CD59","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria"]]]],
{f:"Classical (Ag–Ab) → C1 → C4, C2 → C4b2a\nLectin (MBL) → MASP → C4, C2 → C4b2a\nAlternative (microbe) → C3b + B + D → C3bBb\n → C3 → C3a (inflammation) + C3b (opsonin)\n → C5 → C5a (chemotaxis) + C5b → C5b-9 MAC (lysis)",c:"Complement links innate and adaptive immunity; its pathways converge on C3 and MAC formation, and deficiencies produce characteristic infections and autoimmune diseases."});

Z("micro2",9,"m",["T cells and B cells are the two main lymphocytes of adaptive immunity: T cells mature in the thymus and give cell-mediated immunity; B cells mature in the bone marrow and make antibodies.","B cells are weapon factories (antibodies); T cells are special forces — helpers coordinate, killers destroy infected cells.",["T cells: CD3, TCR, recognise processed peptide with MHC; CD4 helpers, CD8 cytotoxic, regulatory T cells.","B cells: CD19, CD20, surface Ig (BCR), recognise native antigen; become plasma cells and memory cells.","T cells: 65–75% of blood lymphocytes; paracortex of lymph nodes, PALS in spleen.","B cells: 10–15%; follicles and germinal centres."],"B = Bone marrow, antiBodies; T = Thymus, killing & helping."],
"Lymphocytes of the adaptive immune system are B and T cells, morphologically similar but functionally distinct. T lymphocytes originate in bone marrow and mature in the thymus; they bear T-cell receptors (TCR, αβ or γδ) associated with CD3, and recognise processed peptide antigens only when presented by MHC molecules (MHC restriction). Subsets: CD4+ helper T cells (Th1 — IFN-γ, macrophage activation; Th2 — IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, antibody and allergy; Th17 — IL-17, neutrophilic inflammation; Tfh — B-cell help) recognising MHC class II; CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognising MHC class I, killing virus-infected and tumour cells via perforin and granzymes; regulatory T cells (CD4+CD25+FOXP3+) maintaining tolerance. B lymphocytes mature in bone marrow (bursa equivalent); they bear surface immunoglobulin (IgM, IgD) as B-cell receptor with CD79a/b, recognise native unprocessed antigen, and differentiate into antibody-secreting plasma cells and memory cells; they also act as antigen-presenting cells. Markers: CD19, CD20, CD21 (EBV receptor). T cells occupy the paracortex of lymph nodes and periarteriolar lymphoid sheath of spleen; B cells occupy follicles and germinal centres.",
[["Differences",[["Feature","T cells","B cells"],["Site of maturation","Thymus","Bone marrow"],["Proportion in blood","65–75%","10–15%"],["Antigen receptor","TCR + CD3","Surface Ig (IgM, IgD) + CD79"],["Antigen recognition","Processed peptide + MHC","Native antigen (proteins, polysaccharides)"],["Markers","CD3, CD2, CD4/CD8, CD7","CD19, CD20, CD21, CD22"],["Function","Cell-mediated immunity, help, cytotoxicity","Humoral immunity, antibody production"],["Products","Cytokines, perforin","Antibodies"],["Location in node","Paracortex","Follicles, germinal centres"],["Location in spleen","PALS","Follicles (white pulp)"],["E-rosette (sheep RBC)","Positive (CD2)","Negative"],["Tests","CD4/CD8 counts, Mantoux","Serum Ig levels"]]]],
{c:"T and B cells cooperate in adaptive immunity — T helpers enable B cells to produce high-affinity antibodies — and defects in either lead to characteristic immunodeficiencies."});

Z("micro2",10,"m",["MHC (major histocompatibility complex) molecules display fragments of proteins on the cell surface for T cells; in humans they are called HLA and determine transplant compatibility.","MHC molecules are display windows: class I shows what is being made inside every cell (to CD8 killers); class II shows what antigen-presenting cells have eaten (to CD4 helpers).",["Genes on chromosome 6.","Class I (HLA-A, B, C): all nucleated cells, present endogenous peptides to CD8.","Class II (HLA-DR, DQ, DP): APCs (macrophages, dendritic, B cells), present exogenous peptides to CD4.","Class III: complement components, TNF. HLA typing: serological, molecular (PCR-SSP, SSO, sequencing); disease associations (B27 — ankylosing spondylitis)."],"MHC I × CD8 = 8; MHC II × CD4 = 8 ('rule of 8')."],
"The major histocompatibility complex is a cluster of genes (on chromosome 6 in humans — HLA) encoding molecules that present peptide antigens to T cells and determine graft compatibility. Class I molecules (HLA-A, -B, -C) consist of a polymorphic α chain with β2-microglobulin; expressed on all nucleated cells and platelets; present endogenous (cytosolic) peptides — viral or tumour — to CD8 cytotoxic T cells. Class II molecules (HLA-DR, -DQ, -DP) have α and β chains, expressed on professional antigen-presenting cells (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells) and activated T cells; present exogenous peptides processed in endosomes to CD4 helper T cells. Class III genes encode complement components (C2, C4, factor B), TNF and heat-shock proteins. HLA genes are highly polymorphic and codominantly expressed; inherited as haplotypes (siblings have 25% chance of being identical). HLA typing methods: serological (microlymphocytotoxicity), mixed lymphocyte reaction (cellular), and molecular (PCR-SSP, SSO, sequencing — now standard). Applications: tissue and organ transplantation (matching donor and recipient), disease associations (HLA-B27 ankylosing spondylitis, DR3/DR4 type 1 diabetes, DR4 rheumatoid arthritis, DQ2/DQ8 coeliac disease, B51 Behçet, DR2 multiple sclerosis), paternity testing, forensic and anthropological studies, drug hypersensitivity (HLA-B*5701 abacavir, B*1502 carbamazepine).",
[["Class I vs class II",[["Feature","Class I","Class II"],["Loci","HLA-A, B, C","HLA-DR, DQ, DP"],["Structure","α chain + β2-microglobulin","α + β chains"],["Distribution","All nucleated cells","APCs (dendritic, macrophage, B cells)"],["Peptide source","Endogenous (cytosolic)","Exogenous (endocytosed)"],["Presented to","CD8+ T cells","CD4+ T cells"],["Peptide length","8–10 amino acids","13–25 amino acids"]]],
["HLA–disease associations",[["HLA","Disease"],["B27","Ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis"],["DR3, DR4","Type 1 diabetes"],["DR4","Rheumatoid arthritis"],["DQ2, DQ8","Coeliac disease"],["DR2 (DR15)","Multiple sclerosis, narcolepsy"],["B51","Behçet disease"],["B*5701","Abacavir hypersensitivity"]]]],
{c:"MHC molecules underpin antigen presentation, T-cell activation and transplant rejection; HLA typing is essential for organ and stem cell transplantation."});

Z("micro2",11,"m",["The primary immune response is the first, slow, weak antibody response to a new antigen, mostly IgM; the secondary response on re-exposure is quick, strong, long-lasting and mainly IgG because memory cells remember.","First meeting a stranger takes time to recognise them; meeting them again you remember instantly — that's immunological memory.",["Primary: lag 7–10 days, low titre, IgM, short-lived, low affinity.","Secondary (booster/anamnestic): lag 1–3 days, high titre, IgG, long-lasting, high affinity (affinity maturation).","Basis of booster doses in vaccination.","Primary needs more antigen; secondary responds to small doses."],"Secondary = 'Sooner, Stronger, Sustained, Switched (IgG)'."],
"The primary immune response follows the first exposure to an antigen: after a lag (latent) phase of 4–10 days during which antigen is processed and naive B cells proliferate and differentiate, antibody titre rises slowly to a low peak and declines quickly; the antibody is predominantly IgM with low affinity. Memory B and T cells are generated. The secondary (anamnestic or booster) response follows re-exposure to the same antigen: the lag phase is short (1–3 days), antibody rises rapidly to a much higher titre (10–100 fold), persists longer, is mainly IgG (also IgA, IgE) owing to class switching, and has higher affinity due to affinity maturation (somatic hypermutation in germinal centres). Smaller doses of antigen suffice, and the response is less dependent on adjuvants. The negative phase is a transient fall in antibody immediately after a booster due to combination with antigen. This principle underlies booster immunisation schedules and the diagnostic use of IgM (recent infection) vs IgG (past infection or secondary response).",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Primary response","Secondary response"],["Antigen exposure","First","Subsequent"],["Lag period","Long (4–10 days)","Short (1–3 days)"],["Peak titre","Low","High (10–100×)"],["Duration","Short","Long"],["Antibody class","Mainly IgM","Mainly IgG"],["Affinity","Low","High (affinity maturation)"],["Antigen dose needed","Large","Small"],["Responding cells","Naive B cells","Memory B cells"],["Adjuvant need","Often","Less"],["Negative phase","Absent","May occur"]]]],
{f:"Antibody titre\n   │            secondary (IgG, high, fast)\n   │           ╱‾‾‾‾‾‾‾‾‾‾\n   │  primary  ╱\n   │  (IgM) ╱‾╲_____╱\n   └─────────────────────── time\n      1st dose       booster",c:"Immunological memory produces a faster, stronger and more durable secondary response, which is the scientific basis of booster vaccination."});

Z("micro2",12,"m",["Monoclonal antibodies are identical antibodies made by a single clone of cells, all targeting one specific epitope; they are produced by hybridoma technology.","Instead of a crowd of different hunters (polyclonal), monoclonal antibodies are an army of identical snipers aimed at one exact spot.",["Hybridoma technique (Köhler and Milstein, 1975): immunised mouse spleen B cell + myeloma cell fusion with PEG.","HAT medium selects hybrids (myeloma cells lack HGPRT and die; B cells die naturally).","Screen clones by ELISA; expand selected clone.","Uses: diagnosis (pregnancy tests, ELISA, flow cytometry, IHC), therapy (rituximab, trastuzumab, infliximab, adalimumab), imaging and research."],"Monoclonal names: -omab (mouse), -ximab (chimeric), -zumab (humanised), -umab (human)."],
"Monoclonal antibodies are homogeneous antibodies derived from a single clone of plasma cells, directed against a single epitope. Köhler and Milstein (1975, Nobel Prize 1984) developed hybridoma technology: a mouse is immunised with antigen; its spleen B cells (antibody-producing but short-lived) are fused using polyethylene glycol with mouse myeloma cells (immortal, but lacking HGPRT and not secreting their own antibody); hybrid cells are selected in HAT medium (hypoxanthine, aminopterin, thymidine) — unfused myeloma cells die because aminopterin blocks de novo nucleotide synthesis and they cannot use the salvage pathway, while unfused B cells die naturally. Hybridomas are cloned by limiting dilution, screened by ELISA for the desired antibody, and expanded in culture or as ascites. Mouse antibodies cause human anti-mouse antibody reactions, so chimeric, humanised and fully human antibodies (phage display, transgenic mice) are now made. Applications: diagnostic — immunoassays (ELISA, pregnancy tests, rapid tests), immunophenotyping by flow cytometry (CD markers), immunohistochemistry, blood grouping reagents, HLA typing; therapeutic — cancer (rituximab anti-CD20, trastuzumab anti-HER2, bevacizumab anti-VEGF, checkpoint inhibitors nivolumab, pembrolizumab), autoimmune disease (infliximab, adalimumab anti-TNF), transplantation (basiliximab, anti-CD3), infections (palivizumab for RSV, bezlotoxumab for C. difficile), anti-D, toxin neutralisation; imaging, drug targeting and research.",
[["Hybridoma technique",["Immunise mouse with antigen","Harvest spleen B cells","Fuse with HGPRT-deficient myeloma cells using PEG","Select hybrids in HAT medium","Clone by limiting dilution; screen by ELISA","Expand positive clones (culture/ascites); harvest antibody"]],
["Monoclonal vs polyclonal",[["Feature","Monoclonal","Polyclonal"],["Source","Single clone","Many clones"],["Specificity","Single epitope","Multiple epitopes"],["Homogeneity","Uniform","Heterogeneous"],["Supply","Unlimited, reproducible","Variable batches"],["Cross-reactivity","Low","Higher"]]],
["Therapeutic examples",[["Antibody","Target","Use"],["Rituximab","CD20","B-cell lymphoma, RA"],["Trastuzumab","HER2","Breast cancer"],["Infliximab, adalimumab","TNF-α","RA, IBD, psoriasis"],["Bevacizumab","VEGF","Colorectal cancer, retinopathy"],["Palivizumab","RSV F protein","RSV prophylaxis"],["Basiliximab","IL-2 receptor","Transplant rejection"]]]],
{i:"Monoclonal antibodies combine exquisite specificity with unlimited reproducible supply, transforming diagnostics and therapeutics.",f:"Immunise mouse → spleen B cells + myeloma (HGPRT⁻)\n → PEG fusion → HAT selection → hybridomas\n → limiting dilution → ELISA screening → clone expansion\n → monoclonal antibody",c:"Hybridoma technology produced antibodies of a single specificity, now indispensable in laboratory diagnosis and targeted therapy."});

Z("micro2",13,"m",["Hypersensitivity is an exaggerated or inappropriate immune response that damages the body. Gell and Coombs classified it into four types: I (IgE, immediate allergy), II (antibody against cells), III (immune complexes), IV (T-cell mediated, delayed).","The immune system is like a security system that overreacts — sounding alarms at pollen (type I), attacking its own cells (type II), clogging pipes with debris (type III), or sending soldiers late (type IV).",["Type I: IgE on mast cells → histamine; anaphylaxis, asthma, hay fever; minutes.","Type II: IgG/IgM against cell-bound antigens → lysis; transfusion reactions, HDN, AIHA, Goodpasture, Graves, myasthenia.","Type III: immune complex deposition → complement, neutrophils; serum sickness, Arthus reaction, PSGN, SLE.","Type IV: T cells → macrophages/cytotoxicity; tuberculin test, contact dermatitis, granulomas; 48–72 h."],"ACID: Allergic (I), Cytotoxic (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed (IV)."],
"Hypersensitivity refers to injurious immune reactions to antigens in sensitised individuals. Gell and Coombs classification: Type I (immediate, anaphylactic): allergen binds IgE fixed on mast cells and basophils (sensitisation via Th2, IL-4), causing degranulation — histamine, leukotrienes, prostaglandins, PAF — within minutes; examples: anaphylaxis (penicillin, bee sting, peanut), allergic asthma, hay fever, urticaria, atopic dermatitis; atopy is a genetic predisposition to produce IgE. Type II (cytotoxic, antibody-dependent): IgG or IgM against antigens on cell surfaces or matrix cause complement-mediated lysis, opsonisation and phagocytosis, ADCC, or receptor dysfunction; examples: incompatible transfusion, haemolytic disease of newborn, AIHA, ITP, Goodpasture syndrome, rheumatic fever, Graves disease (stimulatory) and myasthenia gravis (blocking). Type III (immune complex): antigen–antibody complexes deposit in vessel walls and tissues, activate complement and neutrophils, causing vasculitis; local form — Arthus reaction (intradermal antigen in immunised animal → oedema, haemorrhage, necrosis in 4–10 h); systemic — serum sickness, PSGN, SLE, PAN, farmer's lung. Type IV (delayed, cell-mediated): sensitised T cells — Th1 activating macrophages (tuberculin reaction, granulomas in TB, leprosy) or CD8 T cells killing cells (contact dermatitis to nickel, poison ivy; graft rejection); reaction peaks at 48–72 h. Immediate (types I–III, antibody-mediated, hours) versus delayed (type IV, cell-mediated, days). The Shwartzman reaction is a non-immunological endotoxin-induced haemorrhagic necrosis (local) or DIC-like reaction (generalised, Sanarelli–Shwartzman) after two doses of endotoxin.",
[["Gell and Coombs classification",[["Type","Mediator","Mechanism","Examples"],["I — Anaphylactic","IgE, mast cells","Degranulation: histamine, leukotrienes","Anaphylaxis, asthma, hay fever, urticaria"],["II — Cytotoxic","IgG, IgM, complement","Lysis, opsonisation, ADCC, receptor modulation","Transfusion reaction, HDN, AIHA, Goodpasture, Graves, myasthenia"],["III — Immune complex","Ag–Ab complexes, complement, neutrophils","Deposition → inflammation, vasculitis","Arthus, serum sickness, PSGN, SLE"],["IV — Delayed","T cells, macrophages","Cytokines, cytotoxicity","Tuberculin test, contact dermatitis, TB granuloma, graft rejection"]]],
["Immediate vs delayed hypersensitivity",[["Feature","Immediate","Delayed"],["Onset","Minutes–hours","24–72 h"],["Mediated by","Antibodies","T cells"],["Passive transfer","By serum","By lymphocytes (transfer factor)"],["Desensitisation","Easy, short-lived","Difficult, long-lasting"],["Examples","Anaphylaxis, Arthus","Tuberculin, contact dermatitis"]]],
["Special reactions",["Atopy: hereditary tendency to IgE responses to common allergens (asthma, eczema, rhinitis)","Arthus reaction: local type III — oedema, haemorrhage, necrosis at injection site in hyperimmunised host","Serum sickness: systemic type III 7–14 days after foreign serum (fever, rash, arthritis, nephritis)","Shwartzman reaction: non-immune endotoxin-induced; local haemorrhagic necrosis or generalised DIC"]]],
{i:"Hypersensitivity reactions use the same mechanisms as protective immunity but cause tissue damage; recognising the type guides prevention and treatment.",x:[["Mechanism of type I",["Sensitisation: allergen → Th2 → IL-4 → IgE class switch → IgE binds Fc ε RI on mast cells","Re-exposure: allergen cross-links IgE → degranulation","Early phase: histamine — vasodilatation, bronchospasm, oedema","Late phase (4–8 h): leukotrienes, eosinophils","Treatment: adrenaline for anaphylaxis, antihistamines, steroids, desensitisation"]]],f:"Type I: allergen + IgE on mast cell → histamine (minutes)\nType II: Ab vs cell antigen → complement/ADCC → cell lysis\nType III: Ag–Ab complexes → deposition → complement + neutrophils → vasculitis\nType IV: sensitised T cell → cytokines/macrophages or CTL → 48–72 h",c:"The four types of hypersensitivity differ in effector mechanism and timing; many diseases involve more than one type, and the classification guides diagnosis and therapy."});

Z("micro2",14,"m",["Allograft rejection is the immune attack on a transplanted organ from a genetically different donor, mainly against HLA antigens. Transplants are classified by the relationship between donor and recipient.","The body treats the new organ as an intruder carrying foreign 'ID cards' (HLA), sending antibodies and T-cell soldiers to destroy it.",["Types: autograft (same individual), isograft/syngraft (identical twin), allograft (same species), xenograft (different species).","Hyperacute rejection: minutes, preformed antibodies (ABO, anti-HLA), thrombosis.","Acute rejection: days–months, T cells (cellular) and antibodies (humoral, vasculitis).","Chronic rejection: months–years, vascular intimal fibrosis, graft atrophy. Prevention: HLA matching, cross-match, immunosuppression (cyclosporine, tacrolimus, steroids)."],"Rejection timing: 'Hyperacute = Hours (minutes), Acute = Days–weeks, Chronic = Years'."],
"Transplantation is the transfer of cells, tissues or organs (graft) from a donor to a recipient. Types: autograft (from one site to another in the same individual — skin graft), isograft/syngraft (between genetically identical individuals — identical twins), allograft (between genetically different members of the same species — kidney transplant), xenograft (between species — pig heart valve). Autografts and isografts are accepted; allografts and xenografts are rejected unless immunosuppression is given. Allograft rejection is mediated mainly by recognition of donor HLA (direct pathway — recipient T cells recognise intact donor MHC on donor APCs; indirect pathway — recipient APCs present processed donor MHC peptides). First-set rejection occurs in 10–14 days; second-set rejection (repeat graft from same donor) is accelerated (5–6 days), showing immunological memory. Clinical types: hyperacute rejection (minutes to hours; preformed antibodies against ABO or HLA; complement activation, endothelial injury, thrombosis — graft becomes cyanotic and mottled; prevented by cross-matching); acute rejection (days to months; acute cellular — CD8 and CD4 T-cell infiltration with tubulitis, endotheliitis; acute antibody-mediated — vasculitis, C4d deposition; reversible with immunosuppression); chronic rejection (months to years; intimal fibrosis of arteries — graft arteriosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy; irreversible). Prevention: ABO compatibility, HLA matching (especially DR), lymphocyte cross-match, immunosuppressive drugs (calcineurin inhibitors cyclosporine/tacrolimus, mycophenolate, azathioprine, steroids, mTOR inhibitors, anti-IL-2R and anti-thymocyte antibodies). Complications of immunosuppression: infections (CMV, opportunists) and malignancies (EBV lymphoma, skin cancer).",
[["Types of grafts",[["Type","Donor–recipient","Outcome"],["Autograft","Same individual","Accepted"],["Isograft (syngraft)","Identical twin","Accepted"],["Allograft (homograft)","Same species, different genotype","Rejected without immunosuppression"],["Xenograft (heterograft)","Different species","Rejected rapidly"]]],
["Types of rejection",[["Type","Time","Mechanism","Pathology"],["Hyperacute","Minutes–hours","Preformed anti-ABO/HLA antibodies, complement","Thrombosis, ischaemic necrosis"],["Acute cellular","Days–weeks","CD8/CD4 T cells","Interstitial lymphocytes, tubulitis, endotheliitis"],["Acute humoral","Days–weeks","Donor-specific antibodies","Vasculitis, C4d deposition"],["Chronic","Months–years","T cells, antibodies, cytokines","Graft arteriosclerosis, fibrosis, atrophy"]]],
["Prevention",["ABO matching and HLA typing","Lymphocyte cross-match","Immunosuppression: calcineurin inhibitors, antimetabolites, steroids, mTOR inhibitors, antibodies","Induction of tolerance (research)"]]],
{i:"Organ transplantation is limited mainly by the immune response to donor alloantigens, chiefly HLA molecules.",x:[["Privileged sites and tissues",["Cornea (avascular), brain, testis, anterior chamber of eye","Cartilage, heart valves — low immunogenicity"]],["Bone marrow transplant","Needs closest HLA match; risk of graft-versus-host disease"]],f:"Donor HLA → recipient T cells (direct/indirect recognition)\n ├ CD4 → cytokines → macrophages, B cells (antibodies)\n └ CD8 → cytotoxic killing of graft cells\n → hyperacute (preformed Ab) / acute (T cells, Ab) / chronic (vascular fibrosis)",c:"Matching, cross-matching and targeted immunosuppression have made allogeneic transplantation routine, though chronic rejection and complications of immunosuppression remain challenges."});

Z("micro2",15,"m",["Graft-versus-host disease occurs when immune T cells in a donor graft (usually bone marrow) attack the recipient's tissues.","Instead of the host rejecting the graft, the graft's soldiers attack the host — a reversed rejection.",["Requirements (Billingham): graft has immunocompetent T cells, host has antigens foreign to graft, host cannot reject graft.","Seen after allogeneic bone marrow/stem cell transplant, transfusion of non-irradiated blood to immunodeficient patients.","Targets: skin (rash), liver (jaundice), GI tract (diarrhoea).","Prevention: HLA matching, T-cell depletion, irradiated blood products, immunosuppression."],"GVHD triad: Skin, Liver, Gut."],
"Graft-versus-host disease (GVHD) results when immunocompetent T cells in a graft recognise and attack the tissues of an immunocompromised recipient. Billingham's criteria: the graft contains immunocompetent cells, the recipient expresses tissue antigens absent in the donor, and the recipient cannot mount an effective response to eliminate the graft. It occurs after allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, liver and small-bowel transplants, and transfusion of non-irradiated blood to immunodeficient patients or to relatives (transfusion-associated GVHD). Acute GVHD (within 100 days) affects skin (maculopapular rash, desquamation), liver (cholestatic jaundice — bile duct damage) and GI tract (diarrhoea, bleeding); chronic GVHD resembles autoimmune diseases (scleroderma-like skin, sicca, liver and lung disease). The graft-versus-leukaemia effect is beneficial. Prevention: close HLA matching, T-cell depletion, immunosuppression (methotrexate, cyclosporine), irradiation of blood products.",
[["Acute vs chronic",[["Feature","Acute","Chronic"],["Onset","< 100 days","> 100 days"],["Organs","Skin, liver, gut","Skin, eyes, mouth, liver, lungs"],["Features","Rash, jaundice, diarrhoea","Sclerodermatous skin, sicca, obliterative bronchiolitis"]]]],
{c:"GVHD is the major complication of allogeneic stem cell transplantation; HLA matching and irradiation of blood products prevent it."});

Z("micro2",16,"m",["Immunosurveillance is the idea that the immune system constantly patrols the body to find and destroy cancer cells before they form tumours.","Like a security patrol that checks every building at night for intruders (abnormal cells).",["Proposed by Burnet and Thomas.","Effectors: NK cells, cytotoxic T cells, macrophages, interferons.","Evidence: more cancers in immunodeficiency (HIV, transplant recipients), lymphocytic infiltrates in tumours, spontaneous regression.","Tumours escape via immunoediting, loss of MHC I, PD-L1, regulatory T cells."],"Immunosurveillance → editing → escape (3 Es: elimination, equilibrium, escape)."],
"Immunological surveillance (Burnet and Thomas) proposes that the immune system recognises and eliminates transformed cells, preventing development of most cancers. Effector mechanisms include NK cells (kill cells lacking MHC class I), cytotoxic CD8 T cells (recognise tumour antigens presented on MHC I), macrophages, antibodies (ADCC) and cytokines (IFN-γ, TNF). Evidence: increased incidence of cancers (especially virus-associated — lymphomas, Kaposi sarcoma, skin cancers) in immunodeficient patients (congenital immunodeficiencies, AIDS, transplant recipients on immunosuppression), increased cancers at extremes of age, lymphocytic infiltrates in tumours correlating with better prognosis, and occasional spontaneous regression. Immunoediting has three phases: elimination, equilibrium and escape. Tumours escape by reducing MHC I expression, losing antigens, expressing checkpoint ligands (PD-L1), secreting immunosuppressive factors (TGF-β), recruiting Tregs and myeloid suppressor cells. These insights led to immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy.",
[["Evidence",["High cancer rates in immunosuppressed individuals","Tumour-infiltrating lymphocytes with better prognosis","Spontaneous regression (melanoma, neuroblastoma)","Effective immunotherapy (checkpoint inhibitors, CAR-T)"]],
["Tumour escape mechanisms",["Loss of tumour antigens or MHC I","PD-L1 expression → T-cell exhaustion","TGF-β, IL-10 secretion","Regulatory T cells, myeloid-derived suppressor cells"]]],
{c:"Immunosurveillance explains why immunodeficiency predisposes to cancer and underlies modern cancer immunotherapy."});

Z("micro2",17,"m",["Blood transfusion can cause immune complications (haemolytic, febrile, allergic, TRALI, GVHD) and non-immune ones (infections, overload, metabolic and iron overload).","Transfused blood is a gift that may bring unwanted extras: mismatched flags (antigens), germs, too much volume or too much iron.",["Acute haemolytic: ABO incompatibility — fever, chills, back pain, haemoglobinuria, shock, DIC, renal failure.","Febrile non-haemolytic: antibodies to donor leucocytes (prevent by leucoreduction).","Allergic/anaphylactic: plasma proteins; IgA-deficient recipients with anti-IgA.","Infections: HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis, malaria (screened), CMV; bacterial contamination (platelets)."],"Transfusion complications 'immune + infection + overload + iron'."],
"Complications of blood transfusion are classified as immunological and non-immunological, acute and delayed. Acute immunological: acute haemolytic transfusion reaction (ABO incompatibility, usually from clerical errors; IgM anti-A/anti-B causing complement-mediated intravascular haemolysis — fever, chills, loin pain, hypotension, haemoglobinuria, DIC, acute renal failure); febrile non-haemolytic reaction (antibodies to donor leucocytes/HLA, cytokines in stored products); allergic reactions (urticaria) and anaphylaxis (anti-IgA in IgA-deficient recipients); transfusion-related acute lung injury (donor anti-HLA/anti-neutrophil antibodies). Delayed immunological: delayed haemolytic reaction (anamnestic alloantibody response, e.g., Kidd, Rh); alloimmunisation; post-transfusion purpura; transfusion-associated GVHD (prevented by irradiation). Non-immunological: transfusion-transmitted infections (HIV, HBV, HCV, HTLV, syphilis, malaria, CMV, bacterial contamination — especially platelets), circulatory overload (TACO), massive transfusion complications (hypothermia, hypocalcaemia from citrate, hyperkalaemia, dilutional coagulopathy), air embolism, thrombophlebitis and iron overload (haemosiderosis in thalassaemia). Mandatory screening in India: HIV 1/2, HBsAg, HCV, syphilis (VDRL/TPHA) and malaria.",
[["Classification",[["Category","Acute","Delayed"],["Immune","Acute haemolytic, febrile non-haemolytic, allergic, anaphylaxis, TRALI","Delayed haemolytic, alloimmunisation, post-transfusion purpura, TA-GVHD"],["Non-immune","TACO, bacterial sepsis, hypothermia, hypocalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, air embolism","Infections (HIV, HBV, HCV, malaria), iron overload"]]],
["Prevention",["Correct patient identification and compatibility testing","Leucoreduced products (febrile reactions, CMV)","Irradiated products (TA-GVHD) for immunocompromised","Washed products for IgA-deficient recipients","Donor screening and NAT testing","Slow transfusion in cardiac patients"]]],
{c:"Most serious transfusion reactions are preventable by meticulous identification, compatibility testing, donor screening and appropriate product selection."});

Z("micro2",18,"m",["Cytokines are small proteins released by cells that act as chemical messengers to control immune responses, inflammation and blood cell production.","Cytokines are the immune system's text messages: 'come here', 'multiply', 'attack', 'calm down'.",["Types: interleukins, interferons, TNF, chemokines, colony-stimulating factors, TGF-β.","IL-1, IL-6, TNF: fever, acute phase response (endogenous pyrogens).","IFN-γ (Th1), IL-4 (Th2), IL-17 (Th17), IL-10 and TGF-β (anti-inflammatory).","Clinical use: interferons (hepatitis, MS), G-CSF (neutropenia), EPO; anti-cytokine drugs (infliximab, tocilizumab)."],"Fever cytokines 'IL-1, IL-6, TNF' = 'Hot T-bone stEAK' (IL-1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6...)."],
"Cytokines are low-molecular-weight soluble proteins or glycoproteins secreted by immune and other cells that regulate immunity, inflammation and haematopoiesis by binding specific receptors. They act in autocrine, paracrine or endocrine fashion, and show pleiotropy, redundancy, synergy and antagonism. Families: interleukins (IL-1 to IL-38), interferons (type I — IFN-α/β, antiviral; type II — IFN-γ, macrophage activation), tumour necrosis factors (TNF-α), chemokines (IL-8/CXCL8, MCP-1 — leucocyte chemotaxis), colony-stimulating factors (GM-CSF, G-CSF, M-CSF, erythropoietin, thrombopoietin) and growth factors (TGF-β). Key functions: IL-1, IL-6, TNF — endogenous pyrogens, acute-phase proteins, inflammation, septic shock; IL-2 — T-cell growth; IL-12 — Th1 differentiation; IFN-γ — Th1, macrophage activation; IL-4 — Th2, IgE switching; IL-5 — eosinophils; IL-13 — mucus; IL-17 — neutrophil recruitment; IL-10, TGF-β — immunosuppression; IL-7 — lymphopoiesis. Clinical relevance: cytokine storm (COVID-19, CAR-T, sepsis), therapeutic cytokines (IFN-α for hepatitis, IFN-β for MS, G-CSF, EPO, IL-2) and anti-cytokine therapy (anti-TNF, anti-IL-6 tocilizumab, anti-IL-1 anakinra, anti-IL-17, anti-IL-5).",
[["Important cytokines",[["Cytokine","Source","Main action"],["IL-1","Macrophages","Fever, inflammation, acute phase"],["IL-2","T cells","T-cell proliferation"],["IL-4","Th2","IgE switch, Th2 differentiation"],["IL-5","Th2","Eosinophil growth"],["IL-6","Macrophages","Acute phase proteins, fever"],["IL-8 (CXCL8)","Macrophages","Neutrophil chemotaxis"],["IL-10","Tregs, macrophages","Anti-inflammatory"],["IL-12","Macrophages, DCs","Th1 differentiation"],["IL-17","Th17","Neutrophil recruitment"],["IFN-γ","Th1, NK","Macrophage activation"],["TNF-α","Macrophages","Inflammation, cachexia, septic shock"],["TGF-β","Tregs","Immunosuppression, fibrosis"]]]],
{c:"Cytokines orchestrate immune responses; both cytokine therapy and anti-cytokine biologics are now important treatments."});

Z("micro2",19,"m",["Immunodeficiency is failure of part of the immune system; primary forms are inherited (born with), secondary forms are acquired later (HIV, malnutrition, drugs).","Primary: a factory built with a missing machine. Secondary: a working factory damaged later by fire (HIV) or shortage of supplies (malnutrition).",["B-cell defects: Bruton XLA (no B cells), CVID, selective IgA deficiency (commonest) → bacterial infections.","T-cell defects: DiGeorge (thymic aplasia, hypocalcaemia) → viral, fungal infections.","Combined: SCID (ADA deficiency, γ-chain defect); Wiskott–Aldrich (eczema, thrombocytopenia, infections); ataxia telangiectasia.","Phagocyte: chronic granulomatous disease (NADPH oxidase), LAD, Chédiak–Higashi; complement deficiencies. Secondary: HIV/AIDS, malnutrition, steroids, chemotherapy, diabetes, splenectomy."],"Infection pattern: B defect → bacteria; T defect → viruses/fungi; phagocyte → catalase-positive bacteria."],
"Immunodeficiency disorders result from defects in one or more components of the immune system, causing increased susceptibility to infections, autoimmunity and malignancy. Primary (congenital) immunodeficiencies are genetic: B-cell (humoral) defects — X-linked agammaglobulinaemia of Bruton (BTK mutation, absent B cells, recurrent pyogenic infections after 6 months), selective IgA deficiency (commonest; sinopulmonary infections, anaphylaxis with blood products), common variable immunodeficiency, hyper-IgM syndrome (CD40L defect); T-cell defects — DiGeorge syndrome (22q11 deletion; thymic and parathyroid aplasia, hypocalcaemia, cardiac defects, viral/fungal infections); combined defects — SCID (X-linked common γ-chain, ADA deficiency; absent T and B function, fatal without stem cell transplant), Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome (X-linked; eczema, thrombocytopenia, infections), ataxia telangiectasia (ATM; IgA deficiency, lymphomas); phagocytic defects — chronic granulomatous disease (NADPH oxidase; catalase-positive organisms, granulomas; NBT test negative), leucocyte adhesion deficiency (delayed umbilical cord separation), Chédiak–Higashi syndrome (giant granules, albinism); complement deficiencies (C3 — pyogenic; C5–9 — Neisseria; C1-INH — angioedema). Secondary (acquired) immunodeficiencies are much commoner: HIV/AIDS, protein-energy malnutrition, ageing, malignancies (lymphoma, myeloma, leukaemia), immunosuppressive drugs (steroids, chemotherapy, biologics), diabetes, renal failure, splenectomy (capsulated bacteria), burns and infections (measles).",
[["Primary immunodeficiencies",[["Category","Disorder","Defect","Infections"],["B cell","Bruton XLA","BTK; no B cells","Pyogenic bacteria, enteroviruses"],["B cell","Selective IgA deficiency","IgA absent","Sinopulmonary, Giardia"],["T cell","DiGeorge","Thymic aplasia","Viral, fungal, PCP"],["Combined","SCID","γc, ADA","All (opportunistic)"],["Combined","Wiskott–Aldrich","WAS protein","Encapsulated bacteria"],["Phagocyte","CGD","NADPH oxidase","Catalase + (S. aureus, Aspergillus)"],["Phagocyte","LAD","CD18","Bacterial, no pus"],["Complement","C5–C9","MAC","Neisseria"]]],
["Secondary causes",["HIV infection","Malnutrition","Drugs: steroids, cytotoxics, biologics","Malignancy: lymphoma, myeloma, CLL","Diabetes, CKD, liver disease","Splenectomy","Ageing, burns, measles"]]],
{c:"The pattern of recurrent infections points to the defective arm of immunity; secondary causes, especially HIV and malnutrition, are far more common than primary defects."});

Z("micro2",20,"m",["Autoimmunity is when the immune system attacks the body's own tissues because self-tolerance breaks down.","The immune system's 'friend-or-foe' training fails, so soldiers fire on their own side.",["Normal tolerance: central (thymus, marrow — deletion) and peripheral (anergy, Tregs, AIRE).","Mechanisms of breakdown: molecular mimicry (rheumatic fever), release of sequestered antigens (sympathetic ophthalmia), epitope spreading, polyclonal activation (EBV), loss of Tregs.","Genetic factors: HLA (DR3, DR4, B27), PTPN22, CTLA4, AIRE; female sex.","Organ-specific (Hashimoto, type 1 DM, myasthenia) vs systemic (SLE, RA)."],"Autoimmunity mechanisms 'MASH': Mimicry, Antigen release (sequestered), Suppressor (Treg) failure, HLA."],
"Autoimmunity is an immune response against self antigens, resulting in autoimmune disease when it causes tissue damage. Normally, self-tolerance is maintained by central tolerance (negative selection of self-reactive T cells in the thymus — AIRE-mediated, and B cells in the marrow — receptor editing) and peripheral tolerance (anergy due to lack of co-stimulation, suppression by regulatory T cells, activation-induced cell death, and antigen sequestration). Mechanisms of breakdown: genetic susceptibility (HLA alleles, PTPN22, CTLA4, AIRE, FOXP3 mutations), environmental triggers such as infections causing molecular mimicry (streptococcal M protein and cardiac myosin in rheumatic fever; Campylobacter in Guillain–Barré), release of sequestered antigens (lens, sperm, sympathetic ophthalmia after eye injury), modification of self antigens by drugs or viruses (drug-induced lupus), upregulation of co-stimulators on APCs during infection (bystander activation), polyclonal B-cell activation (EBV, LPS), epitope spreading, defective Tregs, defective apoptosis (Fas — ALPS), and hormonal influences (female predominance). Autoimmune diseases are organ-specific (Hashimoto thyroiditis, Graves disease, type 1 diabetes, myasthenia gravis, pernicious anaemia, AIHA) or systemic (SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis, Sjögren syndrome). Tissue damage occurs through hypersensitivity types II, III and IV.",
[["Mechanisms",[["Mechanism","Example"],["Molecular mimicry","Rheumatic fever, reactive arthritis, Guillain–Barré"],["Sequestered antigen release","Sympathetic ophthalmia, orchitis"],["Altered self antigen","Drug-induced lupus, AIHA from methyldopa"],["Polyclonal activation","EBV, superantigens"],["Treg defects","IPEX (FOXP3)"],["Failure of central tolerance","APECED (AIRE)"],["Defective apoptosis","Autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (Fas)"]]],
["Classification",[["Organ-specific","Systemic"],["Hashimoto, Graves","SLE"],["Type 1 diabetes","Rheumatoid arthritis"],["Myasthenia gravis","Systemic sclerosis"],["Pernicious anaemia","Sjögren syndrome"],["Goodpasture","Dermatomyositis"]]]],
{c:"Autoimmunity results from interplay of genetic susceptibility and environmental triggers that break self-tolerance; autoantibodies help diagnosis and monitoring."});

Z("micro2",21,"m",["Vaccines train the immune system to recognise germs without causing disease; they can be live attenuated, killed, toxoid, subunit, conjugate, or newer types like mRNA. Immunoprophylaxis also includes passive antibodies.","A vaccine is a wanted poster or a training drill: the immune system learns the enemy's face without facing the real danger.",["Live attenuated: BCG, OPV, MR/MMR, rotavirus, varicella, yellow fever — strong, long immunity; avoid in immunocompromised and pregnancy.","Killed/inactivated: IPV, rabies, hepatitis A, whole-cell pertussis. Toxoids: tetanus, diphtheria.","Subunit/recombinant: HBV (HBsAg), HPV; conjugate: Hib, pneumococcal (PCV), typhoid conjugate. New: mRNA, viral vector (COVID-19).","India UIP: BCG, OPV, HepB birth; Pentavalent, OPV, IPV, Rota, PCV at 6-10-14 weeks; MR, JE, vitamin A at 9 months; boosters at 16–24 months, DPT 5 y, Td 10 & 16 y."],"Live vaccines 'BOMRY-VR': BCG, OPV, MMR, Rotavirus, Yellow fever, Varicella."],
"Vaccines are biological preparations that induce active immunity. Types: live attenuated vaccines (BCG, OPV, measles, MR/MMR, rotavirus, varicella, yellow fever, typhoid Ty21a, cholera oral) — single dose often sufficient, induce humoral and cellular (and mucosal for OPV) immunity, but risk reversion and are contraindicated in immunodeficiency and pregnancy; inactivated (killed) vaccines (IPV, rabies cell-culture vaccine, hepatitis A, whole-cell pertussis, influenza) — safe, need multiple doses and boosters; toxoids (tetanus, diphtheria) — formaldehyde-inactivated exotoxins; subunit and recombinant vaccines (hepatitis B HBsAg, HPV VLP, acellular pertussis); polysaccharide (meningococcal, Vi typhoid — T-independent, poor in < 2 years) and conjugate vaccines (Hib, PCV, typhoid conjugate, MenACWY — T-dependent, effective in infants); newer platforms (mRNA, viral vector, DNA). Adjuvants (aluminium salts) enhance responses. Passive immunisation uses immunoglobulins (HRIG for rabies, HBIG, TIG, VZIG, anti-D, antisnake venom) for immediate protection, often with vaccine (combined immunisation). India's Universal Immunisation Programme schedule: birth — BCG, OPV-0, hepatitis B birth dose; 6, 10, 14 weeks — pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib), OPV, rotavirus, fractional IPV (6 and 14 weeks), PCV (6, 14 weeks; booster 9 months); 9–12 months — MR-1, JE-1 (endemic), vitamin A; 16–24 months — MR-2, JE-2, DPT booster-1, OPV booster; 5–6 years — DPT booster-2; 10 and 16 years — Td; pregnant women — Td (2 doses or booster). Cold chain maintenance (+2 to +8 °C, vaccine vial monitors) is essential.",
[["Types of vaccines",[["Type","Examples","Features"],["Live attenuated","BCG, OPV, MR/MMR, rotavirus, varicella, yellow fever","Strong, lasting; avoid in immunocompromised/pregnancy"],["Killed","IPV, rabies, hepatitis A, influenza","Safe; boosters needed"],["Toxoid","Tetanus, diphtheria","Antitoxic immunity"],["Subunit/recombinant","Hepatitis B, HPV, acellular pertussis","Safe, specific"],["Conjugate","Hib, PCV, typhoid conjugate","Effective in infants"],["mRNA / vector","COVID-19","Rapid development"]]],
["National immunisation schedule (UIP)",[["Age","Vaccines"],["Birth","BCG, OPV-0, Hep B birth dose"],["6 weeks","Pentavalent-1, OPV-1, Rota-1, fIPV-1, PCV-1"],["10 weeks","Pentavalent-2, OPV-2, Rota-2"],["14 weeks","Pentavalent-3, OPV-3, Rota-3, fIPV-2, PCV-2"],["9–12 months","MR-1, JE-1*, PCV booster, vitamin A"],["16–24 months","MR-2, JE-2*, DPT booster-1, OPV booster"],["5–6 years","DPT booster-2"],["10 & 16 years","Td"],["Pregnancy","Td-1, Td-2 / booster"]]],
["Passive immunoprophylaxis",["HRIG/ERIG — rabies","HBIG — newborn of HBsAg+ mother, needle-stick","TIG — tetanus-prone wounds","VZIG — varicella exposure in high-risk","Anti-D — Rh-negative mothers"]]],
{c:"Vaccines are the most cost-effective health intervention; correct vaccine type, schedule and cold chain maintenance ensure protection of individuals and communities."});

Z("micro2",22,"m",["Tumour immunity is the immune response against cancer cells, which display abnormal antigens; immunotherapy boosts this response.","Cancer cells wear altered uniforms (tumour antigens); the immune system can spot them, but tumours try to hide or switch off the guards.",["Tumour antigens: tumour-specific (mutated proteins, neoantigens, viral antigens) and tumour-associated (CEA, AFP, PSA, HER2, cancer-testis antigens).","Effectors: CD8 cytotoxic T cells (main), NK cells, macrophages, antibodies (ADCC).","Escape: loss of MHC I, antigen loss, PD-L1, Tregs, TGF-β.","Immunotherapy: checkpoint inhibitors (anti-PD-1, anti-CTLA-4), CAR-T cells, monoclonal antibodies, cancer vaccines (HPV, HBV prevent virus-related cancers), BCG for bladder cancer."],"Tumour markers: AFP (liver, yolk sac), CEA (colon), PSA (prostate), CA-125 (ovary), hCG (chorio)."],
"Tumour immunity refers to immune responses against tumours. Tumour antigens include products of mutated genes (neoantigens — mutated RAS, p53), overexpressed or aberrantly expressed normal proteins (HER2, tyrosinase), cancer-testis antigens (MAGE), oncofetal antigens (AFP, CEA), altered glycolipids and glycoproteins (CA-125, MUC1), differentiation antigens (CD20, PSA), and viral antigens in virus-induced tumours (HPV E6/E7, EBV). Effector mechanisms: CD8 cytotoxic T lymphocytes (principal), CD4 T cells, NK cells (kill cells with reduced MHC I), activated macrophages, antibodies (complement, ADCC) and cytokines. Tumours escape by loss of antigen or MHC I expression, expression of inhibitory checkpoint ligands (PD-L1), secretion of immunosuppressive cytokines (TGF-β, IL-10), recruitment of regulatory T cells and myeloid suppressor cells, and antigen masking. Clinical applications: tumour markers for diagnosis and monitoring; immunotherapy — checkpoint inhibitors (ipilimumab anti-CTLA-4, nivolumab and pembrolizumab anti-PD-1), monoclonal antibodies (rituximab, trastuzumab), adoptive cell therapy (CAR-T cells for B-cell leukaemia), cytokines (IL-2, IFN-α), BCG intravesical therapy for bladder cancer, and preventive vaccines (HPV, HBV).",
[["Tumour antigens",[["Type","Examples"],["Mutated proteins (neoantigens)","RAS, p53, BCR-ABL"],["Viral antigens","HPV E6/E7, EBV proteins"],["Oncofetal","AFP, CEA"],["Overexpressed","HER2/neu"],["Cancer-testis","MAGE, NY-ESO-1"],["Differentiation","CD20, PSA, tyrosinase"]]],
["Immunotherapy",[["Approach","Example"],["Checkpoint inhibitors","Anti-PD-1 (nivolumab), anti-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab)"],["Monoclonal antibodies","Rituximab, trastuzumab"],["CAR-T cells","Anti-CD19 for ALL/lymphoma"],["Cytokines","IL-2, IFN-α"],["Vaccines","HPV, HBV (preventive); BCG for bladder cancer"]]]],
{c:"Tumours are recognised by the immune system but develop escape mechanisms; immunotherapy aims to restore effective anti-tumour immunity."});
